A||||4||Banzai||Airbrush Touchup||||5||
Z||000000||Banzai||10-10-2001||03:19 PM||||Has anyone used an airbrush to touch up their paint?? I have the skills and an airbrush. I've got a few long scratchs and some small knicks. Do I touch up and then wheel the paint out or vise-versa. Any help would be appreciated.  My existing paint is in great shape besides the scratches. I don't plan on a paint job until next year.<BR>Thanks <BR>Banzai !!!!!!!!!!||||reg||5
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||10-10-2001||07:26 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Banzai:<BR><B>Has anyone used an airbrush to touch up their paint?? I have the skills and an airbrush. I've got a few long scratchs and some small knicks. Do I touch up and then wheel the paint out or vise-versa. Any help would be appreciated.  My existing paint is in great shape besides the scratches. I don't plan on a paint job until next year.<BR>Thanks <BR>Banzai !!!!!!!!!!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey Banzai!<BR>If you are going to paint next year, I would skip touching it up, unless you are afraid it will rust...<BR>Hit it with a buffer and some glaze first and see if it looks good enough to get by for the time being...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroBUZZ||10-10-2001||07:27 PM||||You'll need to get an exact colour match to only paint the scratched area.  If you fill in the scratches with a small brush, then you can rub the area level and topcoat with the airbrush and rub it out to blend with the surrounding paint.<BR>Gary||||reg||7
Z||000003||Kasdan||10-11-2001||06:57 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fierowrecker:<BR><B> unless you are afraid it will rust...<BR>crash...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>umm, rust?  i thought plastic/fiberglass didnt rust?  lol<P>------------------<BR>Tyler Morgan, 1986 GT<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero21.jpg">          <BR>Aftermarket Power Steering<BR>Aim=FieroDriver1986||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||10-11-2001||02:26 PM||||First clean at least a couple feet away from where your touching up. Soapy water, rinsed well. then rub out that whole area by hand with rubbing compound.<BR>Then you can sand it down and airbrush it. go a little farther out on each coat. when its covered, wet down the overspray around it with just laquer thinner in the airbrush. BE CAREFUL the thinner alone runs very ez. Then just buff it after it dries a week or so with rubbing compound.||||reg||1
